# Credential Hygiene

The pen-test report (finding L-5) flagged that an Alchemy mainnet API key
was visible in operator terminal scrollback after a `heroku config -a …`
command. Anything that lives in `heroku config`, `vercel env`, `aws ssm`,
`doppler secrets`, `.env`, or any other secret store is **disclosed to the
viewer** the moment it is printed to a terminal — and to anyone who later
opens the saved scrollback, screen recording, or chat transcript.

Treat every credential as if it might be reused unless you can show it cannot.

## Required behaviour

When inspecting hosted secrets:

- Prefer `heroku config:get NAME --app …` (single key) over `heroku config --app …` (full dump).
- Pipe full dumps through `grep -v` to hide rotation candidates: `heroku config --app foo | grep -v -i 'KEY\|SECRET\|TOKEN\|PASSWORD\|RPC_URL'`.
- If you must see the value (rare), do it inside a tmux pane you will close immediately, never inside Cursor's terminal pane that is captured in agent transcripts.
- Never paste a real secret into chat, a Trello card, a commit message, a code comment, an issue, or a PR description. Reference the env-var name only.
- Never copy a secret into a screenshot or screen recording.

When committing:

- Run `git diff --staged | grep -E -i '(api[_-]?key|secret|token|password|bearer|alchemy|sumsub|rpc.*v2|aws_access|aws_secret|jwt_secret=)'` before every push. Reject the push if anything matches.
- `.env`, `.env.production`, `.env.staging`, `credentials.json`, `auth.json`, `service-account*.json` MUST be in `.gitignore` for every submodule. Verify with `git check-ignore .env` before assuming.
- If a secret slips into a commit (even one that's never pushed), rotate it. Force-push removal is *not* a substitute for rotation — assume it was already mirrored.

## Rotation triggers (mandatory)

Rotate the secret when **any** of these is true:

- It was printed to a terminal during a session that was captured (Cursor agent transcript, asciinema recording, screen share, screenshot in chat).
- It appeared in any commit, on any branch, ever — even reverted, even on a private fork.
- It was emailed, Slack-DM'd, or pasted into a doc that more than two people can read.
- A team member with access has left the company since the secret was issued.
- It is older than 90 days (set a calendar reminder when issuing the key).

## What "rotate" means

- **Provider side**: issue a new key/token in the provider dashboard (Alchemy, SumSub, Brevo, AWS, Heroku attached add-ons, etc.).
- **Config side**: update the env var on every environment that uses it. For Heroku: `heroku config:set NAME=… --app <app>`. For Vercel: `vercel env add NAME production` (and `staging`, `preview`). For local `.env.local`: update and tell the team.
- **Provider side, again**: revoke or destroy the old key. Rotation is not complete until the previous credential cannot authenticate.
- **Audit trail**: write a one-line note in the ticket or a `secrets-rotation.md` log: "2026-04-17 — rotated ALCHEMY_KEY (PENTEST-013); old key destroyed in Alchemy dashboard at $TIME".

